  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "If \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are nonzero numbers such that \\(x=1+\\dfrac1y\\) and \\(y=1+\\dfrac1x,\\) then \\(y\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(1-x\\)",
    "C": "\\(1+x\\)",
    "D": "\\(-x\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Both equations contain reciprocals, so multiply each by its denominator to reveal the same product \\(xy\\). Since \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are nonzero, the given equations become\n\\[\nxy=y+1,\\qquad xy=x+1.\n\\]\nTheir right sides must be equal, so \\(y+1=x+1\\). Subtracting \\(1\\) gives \\(\\boxed{y=x}\\), choice E.",
